        "content": "<p>Good afternoon. While formalizing delay embeddings, I needed to show that strictly increasing transformations preserve ordinal patterns.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"sd\">/-- Composing a tuple with a strictly monotone map preserves its sorting permutation,</span>\n<span class=\"sd\">including the index order used to break ties. -/</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">@[</span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"kd\">]</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">theorem</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sort_comp_strictMono</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">LinearOrder</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">β</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">StrictMono</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kd\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">obtain</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">hmono</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">htie</span><span class=\"o\">⟩</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">eq_sort_iff.mp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">rfl</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">exact</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">eq_sort_iff.mpr</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">⟨</span><span class=\"n\">hg.monotone.comp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hmono</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fun</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hij</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">↦</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">htie</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">j</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hij</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hg.injective</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"o\">)⟩)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">symm</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>It works for arbitrary linear orders, including tuples with ties. I thought it was worth pulling out so the order and tie-breaking argument doesn't have to be repeated.</p>\n<p>Since <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43741\">leanprover-community/mathlib4#43741</a> changes <code>eq_sort_iff</code>, I may need to adjust my proof if that lands first. I think this lemma may be useful to the library as a small PR. Thoughts?</p>",
        "id": 631797591,
        "sender_full_name": "Nelson Spence",
        "timestamp": 1792019528
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"246273\">@Bhavik Mehta</span> Good morning, I saw your discussion on <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43741\">#43741</a> and your ask for lemmas for the sort API. I'm proposing the one above, I could also cover strictly decreasing maps. For <code>StrictAnti g</code>, using dual orders gives:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Tuple.sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Tuple.sortDesc</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>If <code>v</code> is injective, <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43741\">#43741</a>’s reversal theorem then gives:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Tuple.sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">g</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∘</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Tuple.sort</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">v</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Fin.revPerm</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Would you prefer the strictly increasing lemma on its own? And I'll adapt the proof to the revised <code>eq_sort_iff</code> in <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43741\">#43741</a>. I appreciate your feedback.</p>",
